                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
                "dc.description.abstract" : [ {
                  "value" : "Summary of the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987\nThe Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987 was enacted to establish legal services authorities at the national, state, district, and taluk levels to provide free and competent legal services to eligible persons and to organise Lok Adalats for the amicable settlement of disputes. The Act was enacted on 11 October 1987 and came into force on 9 November 1995. It gives effect to the constitutional mandate of equal access to justice, particularly under Articles 14 and 39A of the Constitution. \n1. Objective\nThe Act aims to:\n•\tProvide free and competent legal services to weaker sections of society. \n•\tEnsure that no person is denied justice due to economic or other disabilities. \n•\tPromote equal access to justice. \n•\tOrganise Lok Adalats for speedy and amicable settlement of disputes. \n2. National Legal Services Authority (NALSA) (Sections 3–5)\nThe Act establishes the National Legal Services Authority (NALSA) to:\n•\tLay down policies and principles for legal aid. \n•\tFrame effective legal services programmes. \n•\tMonitor and coordinate the implementation of legal aid schemes. \n•\tPromote legal literacy and legal awareness. \n•\tCoordinate with State Legal Services Authorities and other agencies. \n3. State, District and Taluk Legal Services Authorities\nThe Act provides for:\n•\tState Legal Services Authorities (SLSAs). \n•\tHigh Court Legal Services Committees. \n•\tDistrict Legal Services Authorities (DLSAs). \n•\tTaluk Legal Services Committees (TLSCs). \nThese authorities implement legal aid programmes, organise Lok Adalats, spread legal awareness, and provide legal assistance at their respective levels. \n4. Persons Entitled to Free Legal Services (Section 12)\nFree legal services are available to, among others:\n•\tMembers of Scheduled Castes (SCs) and Scheduled Tribes (STs). \n•\tWomen and children. \n•\tVictims of trafficking or begar. \n•\tPersons with disabilities or mental illness. \n•\tVictims of mass disasters, ethnic violence, caste atrocities, floods, droughts, earthquakes, or industrial disasters. \n•\tIndustrial workmen. \n•\tPersons in custody (including prisons, protective homes, juvenile homes, and psychiatric hospitals). \n•\tPersons whose annual income is below the prescribed limit. \n5. Lok Adalats (Chapter VI)\nThe Act provides for the organisation of Lok Adalats to settle:\n•\tPending court cases. \n•\tPre-litigation disputes. \nFeatures of Lok Adalats:\n•\tProceedings are based on compromise and conciliation. \n•\tNo court fee is payable; any fee already paid is refunded if the matter is settled. \n•\tThe award is deemed to be a decree of a civil court. \n•\tThe award is final and binding on the parties, and ordinarily no appeal lies against it.",
                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
                "dc.identifier.act_id" : [ {
                  "value" : "AC_CEN_3_46_00003_198739_1517807322718",
